没错。

941
00:42:30.530 --> 00:42:34.170
And, you know, Dwight and Michael are like, why are you dressed like that?
然后，你知道的，德怀特和迈克尔就问，你为什么要打扮成那样？

942
00:42:34.370 --> 00:42:38.110
And Andy's like, basically, the tire iron can be a weapon, but he had to dress like a
安迪的意思基本上是，轮胎撬棍可以当武器，但他必须打扮成

943
00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:40.330
mechanic, so it makes sense why he has it.
修理工，这样他拿着它才合理。

944
00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:46.930
I just want to point out that this man came into our office, sat on reception's couch,
我只想指出，这个人走进我们的办公室，坐在前台的沙发上，

945
00:42:47.110 --> 00:42:50.990
which is a direct line of vision to Andy's desk.
那个位置可以直接看到安迪的办公桌。

946
00:42:51.410 --> 00:42:55.250
He had to pass by Andy to get into the conference room.
他必须经过安迪才能进入会议室。

947
00:42:55.250 --> 00:43:02.010
So I guess Angelo Grotti is not, like, super present in the moment.
所以我猜安吉洛·格罗蒂当时并没有特别留意周围。

948
00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:05.890
Like, he makes no connection that he has ever seen Andy before.
他完全没联想到自己以前见过安迪。

949
00:43:06.090 --> 00:43:10.870
He completely buys the fact that Andy is a random mechanic who has come to this lunch.
他完全相信了安迪只是个来参加午餐的普通修理工。

950
00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:11.670
I know.
我知道。

951
00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:12.470
I just want to say.
我只想说。

952
00:43:12.670 --> 00:43:12.930
Yeah.
没错。

953
00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:14.110
It made me laugh.
这让我笑了。

954
00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:18.010
Here's a little couplet of dialogue that when it was said, I thought,
这里有一小段对话，当时听到的时候，我在想，

955
00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:19.390
Rain improvised that, right?
那是雷恩即兴发挥的，对吧？

956
00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:21.910
I know what you're going to say because I checked, too.
我知道你要说什么，因为我也查过了。

957
00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:22.390
Yeah.
没错。

958
00:43:22.710 --> 00:43:23.850
So Michael says, let's go.
迈克尔说，我们走吧。

959
00:43:24.070 --> 00:43:24.470
Come on.
快点。

960
00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:25.910
And Andy goes, should I change?
安迪问，我要换衣服吗？

961
00:43:26.030 --> 00:43:27.450
And Dwight goes, you're wearing loafers.
德怀特说，你穿的是乐福鞋。

962
00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:28.470
Michael's like, forget it.
迈克尔说，算了。

963
00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:29.070
Forget it.
算了。

964
00:43:29.890 --> 00:43:31.110
Not in the shooting draft.
拍摄剧本里没有这一段。

965
00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:32.090
Not in the script.
剧本里没有。

966
00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:34.930
I've got a location alert for you.
我有一个外景地提醒要告诉你。

967
00:43:35.730 --> 00:43:36.510
Let's hear it.
说来听听。

968
00:43:36.730 --> 00:43:39.810
These guys are going to go to the Garlic Press restaurant.
这些人要去一家叫“大蒜压榨机”的餐厅。

969
00:43:39.970 --> 00:43:41.450
That restaurant is fictional.
那家餐厅是虚构的。

970
00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:42.410
We made that up.
是我们编出来的。

971
00:43:42.650 --> 00:43:47.570
However, when I Googled it, I did find a restaurant in Texas called the Garlic Press,
不过，我谷歌了一下，确实在德克萨斯州找到了一家叫“大蒜压榨机”的餐厅，

972
00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:49.190
and the menu looked amazing.
菜单看起来棒极了。

973
00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:53.890
It reminds me of that restaurant we used to have here called the Stinking Rose.
这让我想起我们以前这里有一家叫“臭玫瑰”的餐厅。

974
00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:55.310
Oh, yes.
哦，是的。

975
00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:57.870
And it was, everything was garlic on the menu.
那家店菜单上所有的东西都加了大蒜。

976
00:43:58.030 --> 00:43:59.090
Every single thing had garlic.
每一道菜都有大蒜。

977
00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:00.710
I remember that place.
我记得那个地方。

978
00:44:01.190 --> 00:44:05.310
Well, we shot this at a place called George's Bistro in Valencia.
我们是在瓦伦西亚一家叫乔治小酒馆的地方拍的。

979
00:44:05.630 --> 00:44:09.450
I believe it has since closed, but it was a local Italian favorite.
我相信它现在已经关门了，但它曾是当地很受欢迎的意大利餐厅。

980
00:44:09.450 --> 00:44:15.150
And it was located in the Gateway Village Shopping Center, which is where we filmed all the parking lot scenes.
它位于门户村购物中心，我们所有的停车场场景都是在那里拍的。

981
00:44:15.610 --> 00:44:22.910
So, inside the restaurant, they have this scene where Andy sits down and Dwight comes back from the bathroom,
在餐厅里，有一场戏是安迪坐下，德怀特从洗手间回来，

982
00:44:23.090 --> 00:44:24.690
you know, because he was scoping the place out.
你知道的，因为他在侦察那个地方。

983
00:44:24.690 --> 00:44:26.070
He brings back a roach.
他带回了一只蟑螂。

984
00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:29.750
He brings back a roach motel with a roach in it.
他带回了一个装着蟑螂的蟑螂屋。

985
00:44:29.750 --> 00:44:30.270
Yes.
是的。

986
00:44:31.270 --> 00:44:35.550
And then Andy hits the roach with his tire iron.
然后安迪用轮胎撬棍打了那只蟑螂。

987
00:44:36.170 --> 00:44:36.810
All right.
好吧。

988
00:44:37.150 --> 00:44:39.750
Brent sent in an audio clip about this one.
布伦特发来了一段关于这个场景的音频剪辑。

989
00:44:39.890 --> 00:44:40.790
It's fascinating.
太迷人了。

990
00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:41.790
Here you go.
给你听听。

991
00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:47.570
The creative tension in the shooting of Mafia peaked in that scene in the Italian restaurant
拍摄《黑手党》这一集时，创作张力在意大利餐厅那场戏达到了顶峰，

992
00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:52.010
when Dwight goes into the bathroom to look for a gun behind the toilet, a la The Godfather.
当时德怀特走进洗手间，像《教父》里那样去马桶后面找枪。

993
00:44:52.210 --> 00:44:54.670
He comes back and he says, no gun back there, just this.
他回来后说，后面没枪，只有这个。

994
00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:57.570
And he tips up a roach motel and a roach falls out on the table.
他把蟑螂屋翻过来，一只蟑螂掉在了桌子上。

995
00:44:57.790 --> 00:45:04.970
And in the first take of that, Ed Helms, spontaneously, unscripted, smashed the roach with his wrench.
在第一遍拍摄时，艾德·赫尔姆斯自发地、即兴地用扳手砸死了那只蟑螂。

996
00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:09.950
After the take, the director, Dave Rogers, and I ran onto set and we said, oh, that was so funny.
拍摄结束后，导演戴夫·罗杰斯和我跑上片场说，哦，那太搞笑了。

997
00:45:09.950 --> 00:45:11.230
Do that again in the next take.
下一遍再来一次。

998
00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:15.430
And the actress said, oh, no, no, no, no, no, Ed will not be doing that again.
但那位女演员说，哦，不，不，不，不，不，艾德不会再那样做了。

999
00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:17.190
This has become a cartoon.
这已经变成卡通片了。

1000
00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:22.130
And for eight more takes, Ed never again hit the roach with the wrench.
于是接下来的八遍拍摄里，艾德再也没有用扳手打过那只蟑螂。

1001
00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:26.830
But if you watch the episode, Ed hitting the roach with a wrench is what ends up in the cut.
但如果你看那一集，艾德用扳手拍蟑螂的镜头最终被剪进去了。

1002
00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:34.670
Now, what is going on here is the earnest search by talented comedy people to find that balance
现在，这里发生的是一群才华横溢的喜剧人为了寻找那种平衡而进行的真诚探索，

1003
00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:42.050
between what Greg Daniels, the show creator, would call the plausible and the comical.
介于该剧主创格雷格·丹尼尔斯所说的“合理”与“滑稽”之间。

1004
00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:44.870
If there's too much plausible, it's not funny.
如果太合理，就不好笑了。

1005
00:45:45.030 --> 00:45:47.350
But if there's not enough plausible, it's silly.
但如果不够合理，又会显得很傻。

1006
00:45:47.570 --> 00:45:53.610
And the search for that is not easy, nor is it doable even by good people in real time.
寻找这种平衡并不容易，即使是优秀的人也无法在实时创作中做到。

1007
00:45:53.610 --> 00:45:58.250
There's a certain trial and error element to finding comedy tone.
寻找喜剧基调的过程中，必然存在一定的试错成分。

1008
00:45:59.270 --> 00:46:06.890
So when I heard this clip from Brent, it really made me think about how much trust you put in your director and editor.
所以当我听到布伦特的这段录音时，我真的在思考你对导演和剪辑师的信任有多重要。

1009
00:46:07.730 --> 00:46:14.350
And when you don't have that trust there, it can make you, as an actor, not try things.
而当你没有那种信任时，作为演员，你可能就不会去尝试一些东西。

1010
00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:17.590
And we did have that trust on our set.
而我们在片场确实拥有那种信任。

1011
00:46:17.770 --> 00:46:19.910
And so Ed tried this moment.
所以艾德尝试了那一刻的表演。

1012
00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:22.210
And in the moment, it didn't feel right to them.
但在当时，他们觉得不太对劲。

1013
00:46:22.210 --> 00:46:23.910
So they were like, we're not going to repeat it.
所以他们当时的想法是，我们不会再拍一遍了。

1014
00:46:23.970 --> 00:46:25.310
And we would do that all the time.
我们经常会那样做。

1015
00:46:25.550 --> 00:46:27.590
I would try a reaction to something.
我会尝试对某件事做出某种反应。

1016
00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:29.250
And I would be like, oh, my God, no, no.
然后我会想，天哪，不行，不行。

1017
00:46:29.390 --> 00:46:31.050
OK, no, no, not doing that again.
好吧，不行，不行，再也不那样做了。

1018
00:46:31.050 --> 00:46:33.290
That was like not the right thing.
那似乎不是正确的做法。

1019
00:46:33.290 --> 00:46:37.550
And then you just kind of you hope everyone agrees and it doesn't end up in the cut.
然后你只能希望大家都同意，这样它就不会出现在最终剪辑里。

1020
00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:41.230
But then ultimately, sometimes you're wrong.
但最终，有时你是错的。

1021
00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:43.530
Like it was the right thing to try.
比如那其实是值得一试的正确做法。

1022
00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:49.390
And I just love how our show had all those checks and balances, I guess.
我真的很喜欢我们的剧集拥有所有那些制衡机制，我想是这样。

1023
00:46:49.730 --> 00:46:50.430
Yeah, me too.
是啊，我也是。

1024
00:46:50.730 --> 00:46:52.230
I thought it made perfect sense.
我觉得这完全说得通。

1025
00:46:52.350 --> 00:46:57.310
If a cockroach is on a table, all of a sudden you're going to try to whack it or shoo it off or something.
如果桌上有只蟑螂，你肯定会突然想去拍死它或者把它赶走之类的。

1026
00:46:57.630 --> 00:46:57.970
Yeah.
没错。

1027
00:46:58.290 --> 00:47:00.150
So that reaction felt very natural to me.
所以那个反应对我来说非常自然。

1028
00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:03.870
It's probably why he did it in the first take, because it was a natural instinct.
这可能就是他第一条就那么做的原因，因为那是本能反应。

1029
00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:06.410
And I love that audio clip.
我很喜欢那段音频。

1030
00:47:06.590 --> 00:47:15.370
There's one thing that keeps coming up for me when I hear Brent's voice make a passionate plea about a joke or why something stayed in the episode or the process.
当我听到布伦特充满激情地为某个笑话辩护，或者解释为什么某段内容保留在剧集中，又或是讲述创作过程时，有一件事总会浮现在我脑海中。

1031
00:47:16.030 --> 00:47:19.730
He's reminding me a lot of a youth pastor we had at church.
他让我想起了我们教会的一位青年牧师。

1032
00:47:20.750 --> 00:47:22.130
He's very passionate.
他非常有激情。

1033
00:47:22.190 --> 00:47:27.770
I hear this cadence in his voice that is reminding me of a Sunday worship service a little bit.
我从他说话的语调中听到了一种类似周日礼拜的感觉。

1034
00:47:28.630 --> 00:47:30.130
It's a compliment, Brent.
这是在夸你，布伦特。

1035
00:47:30.370 --> 00:47:31.530
You're very passionate.
你非常有激情。

1036
00:47:31.870 --> 00:47:33.830
I'm riveted by your storytelling.
你的讲述深深吸引了我。

1037
00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:43.290
While the fellows are at the restaurant, Jim's credit card company calls to verify some charges that seem out of the ordinary.
当伙伴们在餐厅时，吉姆的信用卡公司打来电话，核实一些看起来不寻常的消费。

1038
00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:48.370
There are charges in Puerto Rico, and this has caused a red flag at the credit card company.
波多黎各那边有消费记录，这在信用卡公司引起了警报。

1039
00:47:48.370 --> 00:47:49.190
Yes.
是的。

1040
00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.250
So they call Jim's office, and Kevin answers the phone.
所以他们打到吉姆的办公室，凯文接了电话。

1041
00:47:52.570 --> 00:47:56.610
He manages to give Jim's information.
他竟然把吉姆的信息都告诉了对方。

1042
00:47:57.010 --> 00:48:02.590
They think he's Jim, and they're like, well, you're obviously not in Puerto Rico, so we're going to go ahead and cancel the card.
他们以为他是吉姆，就说，好吧，你显然不在波多黎各，所以我们要取消这张卡了。

1043
00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:04.130
Not just any information.
不仅仅是普通信息。

1044
00:48:04.130 --> 00:48:08.050
He finds the home address and the last four digits of his social security.
他找出了家庭住址和社保号的后四位。

1045
00:48:08.050 --> 00:48:18.150
Well, lady, at 9 minutes and 51 seconds, as Kevin's shuffling through all these papers to give away Jim's information, I noticed something.
好吧，女士，在9分51秒的时候，当凯文翻找这些文件来泄露吉姆的信息时，我注意到了一个细节。

1046
00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:24.950
He has a word search fun book open under all this paperwork.
在这些文件下面压着一本单词搜索游戏书。

1047
00:48:24.950 --> 00:48:31.010
And I thought to myself, is this a character thing, or is this a Brian Baumgartner thing?
我当时就在想，这是角色的设定，还是布莱恩·鲍姆加特纳本人的习惯？

1048
00:48:31.690 --> 00:48:36.050
Because I know Stanley always did his crossword puzzles, and that tracks for me.
因为我知道斯坦利总是做填字游戏，这对我来说很合理。

1049
00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:40.250
But I don't imagine Kevin is sitting around doing word searches.
但我无法想象凯文会坐在那里玩单词搜索。

1050
00:48:40.630 --> 00:48:41.270
Are you kidding?
你在开玩笑吗？

1051
00:48:41.430 --> 00:48:42.690
That is so not Kevin.
那根本不是凯文。

1052
00:48:43.290 --> 00:48:46.190
So I was wondering, you sat with him, Angela.
所以我很好奇，安吉拉，你以前跟他坐在一起。

1053
00:48:46.830 --> 00:48:51.070
Did Brian do, like, word searches in his free time in the background?
布莱恩在背景里的时候，会做填字游戏之类的吗？

1054
00:48:51.070 --> 00:48:51.990
Do you remember?
你还记得吗？

1055
00:48:52.750 --> 00:48:57.970
Mostly over in accounting, he was on his computer surfing the internet, because you could not see his screen.
他大部分时间都在会计部那边上网，因为你根本看不到他的屏幕。

1056
00:48:58.170 --> 00:48:59.170
He was very lucky.
他真是太幸运了。

1057
00:48:59.410 --> 00:49:00.150
I know.
我知道。

1058
00:49:00.650 --> 00:49:00.990
Right?
对吧？

1059
00:49:01.190 --> 00:49:03.530
He never got reprimanded for his computer screen.
他从来没因为电脑屏幕的事被训斥过。

1060
00:49:03.730 --> 00:49:11.550
But I imagine over in Jim's glass box, he was visible in so many different ways that maybe he had to do something to pass the time.
但我猜在吉姆那个玻璃隔间里，他的一举一动都暴露在众目睽睽之下，所以他可能得找点事打发时间。

1061
00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:13.130
Very interesting.
非常有意思。

1062
00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:14.790
I thought it was fascinating.
我觉得这太迷人了。

1063
00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:19.750
As I watched the scene, the only thing I could think of was, why, Jim?
当我看到那一幕时，我脑子里想的唯一一件事就是：为什么，吉姆？

1064
00:49:19.750 --> 00:49:25.490
Why would you leave such personal information just laying about your desk?
你为什么要让这么私人的信息随便放在桌子上？

1065
00:49:25.770 --> 00:49:26.630
I agree.
我同意。

1066
00:49:27.110 --> 00:49:28.590
Why not tidy up a little bit?
为什么不稍微整理一下呢？

1067
00:49:28.710 --> 00:49:31.190
Put that stuff away before you go out of town.
在你出城之前把那些东西收好。

1068
00:49:31.450 --> 00:49:32.770
You're check stubs.
那是你的工资存根。

1069
00:49:32.970 --> 00:49:33.170
I know.
我知道。

1070
00:49:33.170 --> 00:49:34.530
People are going to see what you make?
大家都会看到你的收入吗？

1071
00:49:34.670 --> 00:49:36.630
You think that's not going to cause a stir?
你觉得这不会引起骚动吗？

1072
00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:38.050
I agree.
我同意。

1073
00:49:38.550 --> 00:49:40.330
Trust no one in that office.
在那个办公室里，谁都别信。

1074
00:49:41.830 --> 00:49:46.570
Back at the restaurant, when Grotty arrives, he makes them change tables.
回到餐厅那场戏，当格罗蒂到达时，他让服务员给他们换了桌子。

1075
00:49:47.090 --> 00:49:53.110
And I absolutely loved his bit when he says, is this the table you've chosen?
我特别喜欢他那段台词，他说：这就是你们选的桌子吗？

1076
00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:54.590
It's a half booth, half table.
那是一半卡座，一半桌子。

1077
00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:55.850
It doesn't know what it is.
它自己都不知道自己是什么。

1078
00:49:58.530 --> 00:50:00.150
It made me laugh so hard.
